## Dimension-by-Dimension Analysis

### 1. Research (Deep Information Synthesis)

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Highest BrowseComp score (hard-to-find info retrieval). Strong at maintaining precise references across long contexts. Leads GDPval-AA for economically valuable knowledge work (finance, legal, research). | Slower; more expensive per token. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | 94.3% GPQA Diamond (PhD-level science questions) — best in field. 1M production context ideal for literature review and multi-paper synthesis. Native multimodal (text+image+audio+video). | Sometimes mixes up specific details from different document sections. Less precise cross-referencing than Claude. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Broad benchmark visibility. Good at structured document creation from research. Strong tool-use for pulling data from multiple sources. | Quality degrades more noticeably approaching context limits. |
| **o3-pro** | Strongest pure logical deduction. Excels at formal verification and mathematical reasoning. | Extremely expensive ($60/M output). Slow (30s+ responses). Not practical for iterative research loops. |

**Consensus**: For *scientific* research, Gemini 3.1 Pro's GPQA dominance and 1M context at budget pricing is hard to beat. For *professional domain* research (legal, financial, strategic), Claude Opus 4.6 leads by ~144 Elo on GDPval-AA. GPT excels when research requires heavy tool orchestration across external APIs.

---

### 2. Making Large Plans That Stay Good During Execution

This is one of the hardest dimensions to benchmark, and the most relevant to agent orchestration work. A recent benchmark (YC-Bench, Collinear AI, March 2026) tested long-horizon adaptive planning — models acting as startup founders managing resources over a simulated year. The sobering result: **a simple hand-written heuristic beat every frontier model.** Frontier models struggle with long-term reward discrimination (short-term vs. long-term tradeoffs).

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Plans more carefully than predecessors (per Anthropic's own characterization). Context compaction allows summarizing its own context mid-task, enabling longer-running plans without hitting limits. Best intent understanding for ambiguous specs — critical for plans that must survive contact with reality. | Still degrades on truly long-horizon (multi-day) autonomous planning. Conservative — may over-hedge rather than commit to a plan. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Strong at execution speed once a plan exists. Better at terminal/CLI plan-and-execute loops (77.3% Terminal-Bench with Codex CLI). | Plans tend toward over-abstraction and unnecessary complexity. Adds more scaffolding than needed, which compounds over a long execution. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | Capable planning, especially when plan involves multimodal reasoning. Best survival rate on YC-Bench (8/9 vs. rule-based 9/9). | Higher variance — good plans one run, subtly wrong the next with similar prompts. Inconsistency is the core risk for long execution chains. |

**Consensus**: No model is *good* at this yet in a truly autonomous sense. Claude's advantage is that its plans tend to be more conservative and self-checking, meaning they degrade more gracefully. GPT plans execute faster but accumulate unnecessary complexity. The practical mitigation — and what the best practitioners do — is **externalized plan state with checkpoint-based re-planning**, not trusting any model to hold a plan coherently across many steps.

