### 3. Examining and Critiquing Large Technical Projects

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Strongest positioning for debugging, code review, and sustained agentic work inside technical systems. Maintains coherence across full 200K+ without "lost in the middle" degradation. In a 150K-node React repo benchmark, its context compaction created a metadata map and self-checked against it, virtually eliminating memory drift. | 200K standard context is a limit for truly massive codebases (1M is beta/gated). |
| **GPT-5.4** | Better raw performance on terminal/CLI diagnostic loops. Broader tool ecosystem for pulling in external context. Strong on SWE-bench Pro (57.7% vs ~46% for Claude) — which tests multi-language agentic coding. | More verbose critiques with unnecessary abstraction layers. Context coherence degrades sooner. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | 1M production context — can ingest an entire large codebase in one pass. Good at summarization and extracting key patterns. | Gets the gist right but sometimes conflates specific details from different modules. Higher variance on critique quality. |

**Consensus**: Claude is the clear leader for *precision critique* of large technical projects — the kind where you need the model to trace a bug through seven interdependent modules and get the details right. Gemini wins if you need to ingest the *entire* project at once. GPT is strongest for the iterative diagnose-and-fix loop at the terminal level.

