### 4. Modifying Large Project Infrastructure According to a Plan

This dimension is where the scaffold/harness matters as much as the model. Key finding from March 2026 benchmarks: the same Opus 4.5 swings from 45.9% to 57.5% depending on scaffold — a 12-point gap that dwarfs model-to-model differences.

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Best at understanding vague intent and translating it into correct infrastructure changes. Claude Code's agent teams feature enables dispatching sub-tasks. 80.8% SWE-bench (real-world bug fixes/PR creation). Writes cleaner, more maintainable code — less editing needed. | Slower execution. More expensive. |
| **GPT-5.4/5.3-Codex** | Leads Terminal-Bench (CLI execution loops). Better at DevOps/SRE/CI-CD pipeline modifications. Native computer use for interacting with actual interfaces. GPT-5.3-Codex took a step toward Claude-like territory with better git operations and broader task handling. | Writes correct but over-abstracted code. You'll edit GPT outputs to *simplify* them more often. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | Best price-performance ratio for bulk modifications. Leads LiveCodeBench (fresh competitive programming problems — less training contamination). | Inconsistent on refactoring tasks — can produce a perfect implementation one time and a subtly wrong one the next. |

**Consensus**: For infrastructure modifications following a plan, the *agent scaffold* matters more than the model. A mid-tier model in a great harness beats a frontier model in a bad one. That said, Claude produces the most maintainable output, GPT executes the fastest in terminal-centric workflows, and Gemini gives you the most modifications per dollar.

