### 6. Abstraction Quality (Rubber-Meets-Road Implementation)

The gap between a beautiful architecture diagram and working code. This is about whether the model's abstractions actually hold up when you implement them.

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Writes the most implementation-ready abstractions. Code output requires less simplification. Understands when an abstraction is premature and will push back. Strong at maintaining the right level of abstraction for the problem at hand. | Sometimes *under*-abstracts — produces concrete implementations where a shared abstraction would have been better. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Produces more *complete* abstraction hierarchies. Good at generating full interface/protocol definitions. Better at spreadsheet modeling and structured document generation (87.3% on internal spreadsheet benchmark). | Chronic over-abstraction. Adds factory patterns, strategy patterns, and indirection that aren't justified by the problem scope. This is consistently reported across practitioners. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | Strong at *explaining* abstractions (good for documentation). Can reason about abstractions across modalities (diagramming + code). | Abstractions are less battle-tested — more likely to produce something that looks clean but has a subtle impedance mismatch at integration boundaries. |

**Consensus**: Claude produces abstractions that survive implementation most reliably. GPT produces more elaborate abstractions that often need trimming. This maps directly to your context engineering work — when handing off to a coding agent, Claude's output requires less "translation loss" between the spec and the implementation. GPT's output is better when you *want* a fully elaborated architecture document as a reference artifact, but worse when that document is meant to be implemented literally.

