### 7. Self-Assessment

How accurately can the model evaluate its own work and know when it's wrong?

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Better code review and debugging skills to "catch its own mistakes" (per Anthropic). Lower hallucination rate in professional domains. More likely to say "I'm not confident about this" when appropriate. In the 150K React benchmark, its self-checking against its own metadata map virtually eliminated drift. | Can be overly self-critical, hedging on answers it actually got right. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Better at *structured* self-assessment when explicitly prompted with rubrics. Calibration has improved significantly (GPT-5.2 showed 65% fewer hallucinations than GPT-4o). | Less likely to *spontaneously* flag its own uncertainty. Needs to be prompted to self-check. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | Good at identifying when a problem is outside its competence and deferring. | Higher baseline variance means self-assessment is less reliable — it can be confidently wrong. |

**Consensus**: Claude has the best *unprompted* self-assessment. GPT has the best *prompted* self-assessment when given explicit evaluation criteria. For agent workflows, this distinction matters: if your agents are meant to operate autonomously, Claude's spontaneous self-checking is more valuable. If you have explicit validation steps in your pipeline, GPT's structured self-eval may be comparable.

