### 8. Managing Subagent Dispatch

This is the orchestrator role — deciding what to delegate, to whom, and when.

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Native agent teams feature (Claude Code). Strongest vendor positioning for multi-agent orchestration. Anthropic explicitly designed for "sustained agentic work." Conservative dispatch — less likely to fire off a subagent prematurely. | Agent teams feature is still new. Less ecosystem tooling than OpenAI's function-calling infrastructure. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Strongest public benchmark evidence for multi-step tool use and computer interaction. More mature function-calling ecosystem. Tool search feature (47% token reduction) directly benefits orchestration efficiency. | Tends toward breadth over depth in dispatch — may parallelize too aggressively. |
| **Grok 4** | Novel four-parallel-agents architecture — natively designed for dispatch. | Newer, less proven. Limited independent evaluation. |

**Consensus**: Claude is ahead on *polished orchestration features* for subagent dispatch, and its conservative dispatch style reduces cascading failures. GPT has the more mature and flexible tooling infrastructure. The Interconnects AI assessment (Feb 2026) puts it well: Claude is slightly ahead on subagent features, but the space is evolving fast. For your two-tier orchestration architecture with sanitized project profiles, Claude's conservative dispatch and better intent understanding for ambiguous tasks is likely the better fit for the orchestrator role, while GPT subagents may execute terminal-level tasks faster.

---

### 9. Quality of Subagent Dispatch

Not just *whether* it dispatches, but whether the dispatched tasks are well-scoped, well-specified, and lead to good outcomes.

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Better at scoping subtasks appropriately. More likely to include context the subagent actually needs. Understands handoff contracts — can produce explicit artifact schemas for inter-agent communication. | May over-specify, giving subagents less room to find better approaches. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Better at decomposing *terminal-level* tasks (specific commands, specific file operations). Stronger at producing subtasks that are independently verifiable. | Subtask specifications tend to be more verbose, with the same over-abstraction problem noted above. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | Can include multimodal context in dispatch (screenshots, diagrams alongside text specs). Cost-effective for high-volume dispatch where many subtasks run in parallel. | Subtask quality is more variable — some are well-scoped, others are ambiguous. |

**Consensus**: Claude produces the highest-quality individual dispatch artifacts. GPT produces more *granular* decompositions that are easier to verify independently. This maps to your handoff contract system: Claude is better at writing the contracts themselves, while GPT may be better as the executor receiving them.

---

### 10. Following a Set of Instructions Without Forgetting Them

This tests instruction adherence over extended interactions — critical for agent workflows.

| Model | Strength | Weakness |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Claude Opus 4.6** | Context compaction explicitly designed for this — can summarize its own context to maintain instruction fidelity over longer runs. 76% MRCR v2 (long-context coherence metric). Maintains coherence across full 200K without "lost in the middle" degradation. | Compaction is lossy — some nuance from original instructions may be compressed away. |
| **GPT-5.4** | Strong system instruction control. Better at *structured* instruction following when instructions are formatted as schemas or rubrics. | Coherence degrades more noticeably as context length increases. After ~90K tokens, earlier instructions start getting deprioritized. |
| **Gemini 3.1 Pro** | 1M production context means instructions rarely get pushed out by sheer volume. Strong at maintaining format/schema adherence. | May maintain the *letter* of instructions but drift on *intent* over long conversations. Detail mixing between context sections. |

**Consensus**: Claude is strongest at maintaining instruction *intent* over long interactions. Gemini is strongest at maintaining instruction *presence* (they simply don't get pushed out of context). GPT is strongest when instructions are highly structured (schemas, rubrics) but weakest when they're narrative. For your `.agent/` documentation scaffold with structured preamble templates, Claude's intent-preservation and GPT's schema-adherence are both relevant — the choice depends on how structured your instruction format is.

